{
  "concept": "Multiplying a vector by a scalar k stretches or shrinks its length by |k| while keeping the same line of action — and reverses its direction when k is negative.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Four parallel arrows stacked vertically, all drawn at the same shallow upward-right slope (roughly 15–20°)",
    "Small filled black dot marking the tail (initial point) of each arrow",
    "Dark navy arrow, longest, labeled '3v' in bold italic at its arrowhead (top of stack)",
    "Orange arrow of reference length, labeled 'v' in bold italic near its head (second from top)",
    "Teal arrow, half the reference length, labeled '½v' (fraction stacked) near its head (third)",
    "Red arrow equal in length to v but reversed: black dot at the right end, arrowhead at the lower-left, labeled '−v' to the right of the dot (bottom)",
    "Optional overlay mode: a single shared tail dot with all scaled arrows radiating along one common line of action",
    "Optional faint dashed guide line through the shared line of action in overlay mode",
    "Optional readout showing the current scalar k, the resulting vector's component form, and its magnitude |k||v|",
    "Optional tick marks / faint length ruler along the line of action showing multiples of |v|"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "scalarK",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That the arrow's length is |k| times the original, that direction is preserved for k>0 and flipped for k<0, and that the vector collapses to a point at k=0.",
      "label": "Scalar multiplier k",
      "range": [-3, 3, 0.25],
      "default": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "layoutMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the stacked view makes lengths easy to compare, while the shared-tail overlay makes it obvious that every scalar multiple lies on one line through the tail — same ray for positive k, opposite ray for negative k.",
      "label": "Arrow layout",
      "options": ["Stacked", "Shared tail overlay"],
      "default": "Stacked"
    },
    {
      "id": "showKArrow",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Lets the learner isolate the live kv arrow against the fixed reference set 3v, v, ½v, −v to check where their scaled vector lands.",
      "label": "Show live kv arrow",
      "default": true
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The reference vector v",
      "narration": "Start with the orange arrow v and its three relatives stacked above and below it. Every arrow points along the same shallow upward slope, so only length and direction-sign differ.",
      "state": { "scalarK": 1, "layoutMode": "Stacked", "showKArrow": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Stretching: k = 3",
      "narration": "Drag k up to 3 and the live arrow grows to three times the length of v, matching the navy 3v arrow exactly — but its heading never changes.",
      "state": { "scalarK": 3, "layoutMode": "Stacked", "showKArrow": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrinking: k = ½",
      "narration": "Pull k down to one half and the arrow contracts onto the teal ½v. A scalar between 0 and 1 shortens the vector without tilting it.",
      "state": { "scalarK": 0.5, "layoutMode": "Stacked", "showKArrow": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Crossing zero",
      "narration": "At k = 0 the arrow shrinks to a single point at the tail — the zero vector. Nudging k just below zero makes the arrowhead pop out on the opposite side.",
      "state": { "scalarK": 0, "layoutMode": "Stacked", "showKArrow": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reversing: k = −1",
      "narration": "At k = −1 the arrow has the same length as v but points down and to the left, matching the red −v arrow. Negative scalars reverse direction along the same line.",
      "state": { "scalarK": -1, "layoutMode": "Stacked", "showKArrow": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Overlay: one line of action",
      "narration": "Switch to the shared-tail overlay: all the arrows now spring from one dot. Positive multiples stack along one ray, the negative multiple lies on the opposite ray — every scalar multiple of v lives on a single line.",
      "state": { "scalarK": -2, "layoutMode": "Shared tail overlay", "showKArrow": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Scalar multiplication changes only how far you travel along a fixed line — |k| sets the length, and the sign of k picks which of the two directions along that line you go.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "White background, four arrows stacked from top to bottom, all parallel at a shallow upward-right slope of roughly 15–20°, each beginning at a small filled black dot. Top: dark navy arrow spanning nearly the full width, labeled '3v' in bold italic just past its arrowhead at upper right. Second: orange arrow about one third that length, labeled 'v' in bold italic above its head. Third: teal arrow half the orange one's length, labeled '½v' with a stacked fraction above its head. Bottom: red arrow equal in length to the orange one but reversed — black dot on the right, arrowhead at lower left, label '−v' just right of the dot. Keep arrowheads solid and proportional to line weight; line weight is uniform across all four. The live kv arrow should be drawn in a distinct accent color (e.g. purple) with a small 'kv' label and its tail on the same black dot as v in stacked mode. In overlay mode, collapse all tails onto one dot near the lower-left with a faint dashed guide line extended through it in both directions; arrowheads then terminate at distances proportional to their scale factors, positive multiples to the upper right and negative ones to the lower left. A small text readout of k and |kv| may sit in a corner."
}

