        "scalarK": -1,
        "layoutMode": "Stacked",
        "showKArrow": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Overlay: one line of action",
      "narration": "Switch to the shared-tail overlay: all the arrows now spring from one dot. Positive multiples stack along one ray, the negative multiple lies on the opposite ray — every scalar multiple of v lives on a single line.",
      "state": {
        "scalarK": -2,
        "layoutMode": "Shared tail overlay",
        "showKArrow": true
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Scalar multiplication changes only how far you travel along a fixed line — |k| sets the length, and the sign of k picks which of the two directions along that line you go.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "White background, four arrows stacked from top to bottom, all parallel at a shallow upward-right slope of roughly 15–20°, each beginning at a small filled black dot. Top: dark navy arrow spanning nearly the full width, labeled '3v' in bold italic just past its arrowhead at upper right. Second: orange arrow about one third that length, labeled 'v' in bold italic above its head. Third: teal arrow half the orange one's length, labeled '½v' with a stacked fraction above its head. Bottom: red arrow equal in length to the orange one but reversed — black dot on the right, arrowhead at lower left, label '−v' just right of the dot. Keep arrowheads solid and proportional to line weight; line weight is uniform across all four. The live kv arrow should be drawn in a distinct accent color (e.g. purple) with a small 'kv' label and its tail on the same black dot as v in stacked mode. In overlay mode, collapse all tails onto one dot near the lower-left with a faint dashed guide line extended through it in both directions; arrowheads then terminate at distances proportional to their scale factors, positive multiples to the upper right and negative ones to the lower left. A small text readout of k and |kv| may sit in a corner."
}

